      "text": "In 337 CE, King Mirian III of Iberia declared Christianity as the state religion, making Georgia one of the earliest countries to do so.",
      "date": "0337"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of King David IV",
      "text": "King David IV, also known as David the Builder, reigned from 1089 to 1125. He is credited with unifying Georgia and initiating the Georgian Golden Age.",
      "date": "1089"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Georgian Golden Age",
      "text": "The Georgian Golden Age, a period of political strength and cultural flourishing, occurred during the 11th to 13th centuries, particularly under the reigns of David IV and Queen Tamar.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Queen Tamar",
      "text": "Queen Tamar, ruling from 1184 to 1213, was the first woman to rule Georgia in her own right. Her reign is considered the zenith of the Georgian Golden Age.",
      "date": "1184"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mongol Invasions",
      "text": "In the 13th century, Georgia faced invasions by the Mongols, leading to the decline of the Georgian Kingdom.",
      "date": "1220"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Annexation by the Russian Empire",
      "text": "In 1801, the Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti (eastern Georgia) was annexed by the Russian Empire, marking the beginning of Russian control over Georgia.",
      "date": "1801"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Democratic Republic of Georgia",
      "text": "Georgia declared independence from Russia on May 26, 1918, establishing the Democratic Republic of Georgia.",
      "date": "1918-05-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Soviet Invasion and Incorporation",
      "text": "In 1921, the Red Army invaded Georgia, and by 1922, Georgia was incorporated into the Soviet Union as part of the Transcaucasian SFSR.",
      "date": "1921"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Restoration of Independence",
      "text": "Georgia declared independence from the Soviet Union on April 9, 1991, following a referendum held on March 31, 1991.",
      "date": "1991-04-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rose Revolution",
      "text": "In November 2003, the Rose Revolution led to the resignation of President Eduard Shevardnadze and marked a peaceful transition of power.",
