      "headline": "Launch of Macintosh",
      "text": "On January 24, 1984, Apple launched the Macintosh, the first mass-market personal computer featuring an integral graphical user interface and mouse.",
      "date": "1984-01-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Departure from Apple",
      "text": "In 1985, following a power struggle with the company's board and its then-CEO John Sculley, Jobs was ousted from Apple.",
      "date": "1985"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding of NeXT",
      "text": "In 1985, after leaving Apple, Jobs founded NeXT Inc., a computer platform development company specializing in the higher-education and business markets.",
      "date": "1985"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Acquisition of Pixar",
      "text": "In 1986, Jobs acquired the computer graphics division of Lucasfilm Ltd, which was spun off as Pixar Animation Studios.",
      "date": "1986"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to Apple",
      "text": "In 1997, Apple acquired NeXT, bringing Jobs back to the company. He became the de facto chief after the resignation of then-CEO Gil Amelio.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of iMac",
      "text": "In 1998, Apple introduced the iMac, a line of personal computers that helped revive the company's fortunes.",
