      "text": "Georgia has 16 electoral votes in the Electoral College. These votes were contested between the Republican Party's nominee, incumbent President Donald Trump, and the Democratic Party's nominee, former Vice President Joe Biden.",
      "date": "2020-11-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Certification of Results",
      "text": "On November 20, 2020, Georgia certified its election results, confirming Joe Biden as the winner of the state's 16 electoral votes.",
      "date": "2020-11-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recount Requested",
      "text": "Following the certification, a recount was requested by the Trump campaign. The recount was completed on December 7, 2020, reaffirming Joe Biden's victory in Georgia.",
      "date": "2020-12-07"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "First Human Settlement",
      "text": "The first known human settlement in Malta dates back to around 5900 BCE, when settlers from Sicily arrived on the islands.",
      "date": "-5900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Temple Period",
      "text": "Between 3600 BCE and 2500 BCE, Malta saw the construction of megalithic temples, such as Ħaġar Qim and Mnajdra, which are among the oldest free-standing structures in the world.",
      "date": "-3600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Phoenician Settlement",
      "text": "Around 800 BCE, the Phoenicians began to settle in Malta, using it as a trading post.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carthaginian Rule",
      "text": "In 480 BCE, Malta came under the control of Carthage, following the decline of Phoenician influence.",
      "date": "-0480"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman Conquest",
      "text": "In 218 BCE, Malta was conquered by the Roman Republic during the Second Punic War.",
      "date": "-0218"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Byzantine Rule",
      "text": "After the fall of the Western Roman Empire, Malta became part of the Byzantine Empire around 535 CE.",
      "date": "0535"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arab Conquest",
      "text": "In 870 CE, the Aghlabids from North Africa conquered Malta, introducing new agricultural practices and the Arabic language.",
      "date": "0870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Norman Conquest",
      "text": "In 1091, Count Roger I of Sicily captured Malta, beginning the period of Norman rule.",
      "date": "1091"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Knights of St. John",
      "text": "In 1530, Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, granted Malta to the Knights of St. John, who fortified the islands and built the city of Valletta.",
      "date": "1530"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Siege of Malta",
      "text": "In 1565, the Ottoman Empire laid siege to Malta, but the Knights of St. John successfully defended the islands.",
      "date": "1565"
    },
    {
      "headline": "French Occupation",
      "text": "In 1798, Napoleon Bonaparte's forces occupied Malta during the French Revolutionary Wars.",
      "date": "1798"
    },
    {
      "headline": "British Rule",
      "text": "In 1814, Malta officially became a British colony following the Treaty of Paris.",
      "date": "1814"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Independence",
      "text": "Malta gained independence from the United Kingdom on September 21, 1964.",
      "date": "1964-09-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Republic of Malta",
      "text": "On December 13, 1974, Malta became a republic, with the President as head of state.",
      "date": "1974-12-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "EU Membership",
      "text": "Malta joined the European Union on May 1, 2004.",
      "date": "2004-05-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "European Capital of Culture",
      "text": "In 2018, Valletta was designated as the European Capital of Culture.",
      "date": "2018"
    }
  ]
}

