      "headline": "First Eurovision Song Contest",
      "text": "The first Eurovision Song Contest was held on May 24, 1956, in Lugano, Switzerland. Seven countries participated, each submitting two songs.",
      "date": "1956-05-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Voting System",
      "text": "In 1957, the Eurovision Song Contest introduced a new voting system where each country had a jury of ten members, each awarding one point to their favorite song.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Contest with a Tie",
      "text": "In 1969, the contest ended with a four-way tie between Spain, the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, and France. No tie-breaking rule was in place at the time.",
      "date": "1969"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Satellite Broadcast",
      "text": "In 1968, the Eurovision Song Contest was broadcast live via satellite for the first time, reaching audiences in Asia and South America.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Contest in Eastern Europe",
      "text": "In 1994, the Eurovision Song Contest was held in Dublin, Ireland, marking the first time Eastern European countries participated following the fall of the Iron Curtain.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Televoting",
      "text": "In 1997, televoting was introduced in five countries, allowing the public to vote for their favorite songs via telephone.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Semi-Finals Introduced",
      "text": "In 2004, due to the increasing number of participating countries, the contest introduced a semi-final round to determine the finalists.",
      "date": "2004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Contest in Australia",
      "text": "In 2015, Australia was invited to participate in the Eurovision Song Contest as a special guest to celebrate the contest's 60th anniversary.",
      "date": "2015"
