      "text": "On May 5, 1789, the Estates General was convened by King Louis XVI in Versailles to address the financial crisis facing France.",
      "date": "1789-05-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tennis Court Oath",
      "text": "On June 20, 1789, members of the Third Estate, who had begun to call themselves the National Assembly, took the Tennis Court Oath, vowing not to separate until a new constitution was established.",
      "date": "1789-06-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Storming of the Bastille",
      "text": "On July 14, 1789, revolutionaries stormed the Bastille prison in Paris, a symbol of royal authority, marking the beginning of the French Revolution.",
      "date": "1789-07-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen",
      "text": "On August 26, 1789, the National Assembly adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen, proclaiming the principles of liberty, equality, and fraternity.",
      "date": "1789-08-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Women's March on Versailles",
      "text": "On October 5, 1789, thousands of Parisian women marched to Versailles, demanding bread and forcing the royal family to move to Paris.",
      "date": "1789-10-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Flight to Varennes",
      "text": "On June 20-21, 1791, King Louis XVI and his family attempted to flee Paris but were captured in Varennes, leading to increased tensions and distrust.",
      "date": "1791-06-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Proclamation of the First French Republic",
      "text": "On September 21, 1792, the National Convention proclaimed the abolition of the monarchy and the establishment of the First French Republic.",
      "date": "1792-09-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Execution of King Louis XVI",
      "text": "On January 21, 1793, King Louis XVI was executed by guillotine in Paris, marking a significant turning point in the Revolution.",
      "date": "1793-01-21"
    },
    {
