      "text": "In 1687, Newton published his landmark work 'Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica,' which laid the foundations for classical mechanics.",
      "date": "1687"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Newton Elected President of the Royal Society",
      "text": "In 1703, Isaac Newton was elected President of the Royal Society, a position he held until his death.",
      "date": "1703"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Opticks'",
      "text": "In 1704, Newton published 'Opticks,' a book that detailed his experiments and theories on the nature of light and color.",
      "date": "1704"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Newton's Dispute with Leibniz",
      "text": "In the early 18th century, Newton became embroiled in a dispute with Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz over the invention of calculus, which both had developed independently.",
      "date": "1710"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Isaac Newton",
      "text": "Isaac Newton died on March 31, 1727, in Kensington, London, England, leaving behind a legacy as one of the most influential scientists in history.",
