      "headline": "Independence and Economic Model Adoption",
      "text": "In 1947, India gained independence from British rule. Following independence, successive governments adopted the Soviet model, promoting protectionist economic policies characterized by state intervention and economic regulation.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Licence Raj",
      "text": "From 1947 until 1991, India followed a system known as the Licence Raj, which involved extensive regulation and licensing requirements for businesses, reflecting dirigism.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nationalization of Key Industries",
      "text": "In the mid-1950s, India effectively nationalized key industries, including railways, highways, banking, insurance, and essential utilities, with the state exerting substantial control.",
      "date": "1955"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Balance of Payments Crisis",
      "text": "In 1991, India faced an acute balance of payments crisis, which led to significant economic challenges and necessitated a change in economic policy.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Economic Liberalisation",
      "text": "In response to the 1991 crisis, India adopted broad economic liberalisation policies, moving away from the Soviet model towards a more open and market-oriented economy.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emergence of Corporate Players",
      "text": "Post-liberalisation, India saw the emergence of key corporate players, particularly in sectors like digitalization, broadband, telecommunication, and space, which had been under state control.",
      "date": "1991"
    }
  ]
}

