    },
    {
      "headline": "Thanksgiving Becomes an Annual Tradition",
      "text": "In 1863, during the American Civil War, President Abraham Lincoln proclaimed a national Thanksgiving Day to be celebrated on the final Thursday in November. This proclamation was influenced by the persistent campaign of Sarah Josepha Hale.",
      "date": "1863"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Franksgiving Controversy",
      "text": "In 1939, President Franklin D. Roosevelt moved Thanksgiving up one week to extend the holiday shopping season. This decision was met with opposition, leading to the term 'Franksgiving.'",
      "date": "1939"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Thanksgiving Date Fixed",
      "text": "In 1941, the U.S. Congress passed a law making Thanksgiving the fourth Thursday in November, where it remains today.",
      "date": "1941"
