      "headline": "Portuguese Control",
      "text": "In 1534, the Portuguese took control of the islands from the Sultanate of Gujarat.",
      "date": "1534"
    },
    {
      "headline": "British East India Company Acquires Bombay",
      "text": "In 1661, as part of the dowry of Catherine of Braganza when she married Charles II of England, the islands were ceded to the British Crown. In 1668, they were leased to the British East India Company.",
      "date": "1668"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bombay Presidency Established",
      "text": "In 1687, the British East India Company transferred its headquarters from Surat to Bombay, marking the beginning of the city's transformation into a major trading town.",
      "date": "1687"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hornby Vellard Project",
      "text": "In 1782, the Hornby Vellard project was started to reclaim the land between the seven islands. The project was completed in 1784.",
      "date": "1784"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Railway in India",
      "text": "On 16 April 1853, the first passenger train in India ran from Bori Bunder in Bombay to Thane.",
      "date": "1853-04-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bombay Presidency Reforms",
      "text": "In 1864, the Bombay Municipal Corporation was established, and in 1869, the Suez Canal opened, making Bombay one of the most important ports in the Arabian Sea.",
      "date": "1864"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bombay Presidency Reforms",
      "text": "In 1869, the Suez Canal opened, making Bombay one of the most important ports in the Arabian Sea.",
      "date": "1869"
    },
