      "headline": "Authorization and Planning",
      "text": "In the late 1980s, the Federal Bureau of Prisons began planning for a new high-security prison to house the most dangerous inmates in the federal system. This was in response to increasing violence and security issues in existing facilities.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction Begins",
      "text": "Construction of the United States Penitentiary, Administrative Maximum Facility (ADX Florence) began in 1990. The facility was designed to be the most secure prison in the federal system.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "ADX Florence Opens",
      "text": "ADX Florence officially opened in November 1994. It was built to house inmates who are deemed too dangerous for even maximum-security prisons.",
      "date": "1994-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Inmates Arrive",
      "text": "The first inmates were transferred to ADX Florence shortly after its opening in 1994. These included some of the most notorious and dangerous criminals in the United States.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "High-Profile Inmates",
      "text": "Over the years, ADX Florence has housed several high-profile inmates, including terrorists, organized crime figures, and other notorious criminals. This includes figures like Ted Kaczynski (the Unabomber) and Ramzi Yousef (1993 World Trade Center bombing).",
      "date": "1990s-2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Legal and Human Rights Challenges",
      "text": "Throughout its history, ADX Florence has faced legal challenges and criticism from human rights organizations regarding the conditions of confinement and the mental health impact on inmates.",
      "date": "2000s-Present"
    }
  ]
}

