      "text": "Harvard University was founded on October 28, 1636, by vote of the Great and General Court of the Massachusetts Bay Colony. It is the oldest institution of higher learning in the United States.",
      "date": "1636-10-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Harvard College",
      "text": "In 1639, the institution was named Harvard College after its first benefactor, John Harvard, a young clergyman who bequeathed his library and half of his estate to the college.",
      "date": "1639"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Harvard's First Commencement",
      "text": "Harvard held its first commencement in 1642, with nine graduates.",
      "date": "1642"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Harvard Corporation",
      "text": "In 1650, the Harvard Corporation, the oldest corporation in the Western Hemisphere, was established to govern the institution.",
      "date": "1650"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Harvard Hall Fire",
      "text": "In 1764, a fire destroyed Harvard Hall, including the college library and scientific equipment.",
      "date": "1764"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Harvard Medical School Founded",
      "text": "Harvard Medical School was founded in 1782, expanding the university's academic offerings.",
      "date": "1782"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Harvard Law School Founded",
      "text": "In 1817, Harvard Law School was established, becoming one of the first professional law schools in the United States.",
      "date": "1817"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Radcliffe College Established",
      "text": "In 1879, Radcliffe College was established as a women's college affiliated with Harvard University.",
      "date": "1879"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Integration of Radcliffe College",
      "text": "In 1999, Radcliffe College was formally integrated into Harvard University, becoming the Radcliffe Institute for Advanced Study.",
