    {
      "headline": "Publication of the Dunning–Kruger Effect",
      "text": "In 1999, the Dunning–Kruger effect was first described by psychologists David Dunning and Justin Kruger in their paper published in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology. The paper was titled 'Unskilled and Unaware of It: How Difficulties in Recognizing One's Own Incompetence Lead to Inflated Self-Assessments.'",
      "date": "1999"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition and Popularization",
      "text": "Following its publication, the Dunning–Kruger effect gained recognition in both academic circles and popular culture. It became a widely referenced concept in discussions about cognitive biases and human psychology.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Further Research and Studies",
      "text": "Over the years, numerous studies have been conducted to explore and expand upon the Dunning–Kruger effect. Researchers have examined its implications in various fields, including education, business, and politics.",
      "date": "2000-2020"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Misinterpretations in Popular Culture",
      "text": "Despite its academic origins, the Dunning–Kruger effect is often misunderstood in popular culture. It is sometimes incorrectly cited as a general statement about the overconfidence of people with low intelligence, rather than a specific bias related to unskilled individuals overestimating their abilities.",
      "date": "2000-2023"
    }
  ]
}

