      "headline": "Death of Hu Yaobang",
      "text": "On April 15, 1989, former General Secretary of the Communist Party of China, Hu Yaobang, passed away. His death sparked the initial student gatherings in Tiananmen Square, as he was seen as a reformist leader.",
      "date": "1989-04-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Beginning of Protests",
      "text": "On April 18, 1989, students began gathering in Tiananmen Square to mourn Hu Yaobang and to call for political reform, marking the start of the protests.",
      "date": "1989-04-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "April 26 Editorial",
      "text": "On April 26, 1989, the People's Daily published an editorial that condemned the protests as 'turmoil', which escalated tensions between the government and the protesters.",
      "date": "1989-04-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "May Fourth Movement Anniversary",
      "text": "On May 4, 1989, students marched to commemorate the 70th anniversary of the May Fourth Movement, demanding greater freedom of speech and political reform.",
      "date": "1989-05-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hunger Strike Begins",
      "text": "On May 13, 1989, a group of students began a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square to press for dialogue with the government.",
      "date": "1989-05-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gorbachev's Visit to China",
      "text": "On May 15, 1989, Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visited China, drawing international media attention to the protests.",
      "date": "1989-05-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Martial Law Declared",
      "text": "On May 20, 1989, the Chinese government declared martial law in Beijing in an attempt to control the escalating protests.",
      "date": "1989-05-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Goddess of Democracy",
      "text": "On May 30, 1989, protesters erected a 10-meter tall statue called the 'Goddess of Democracy' in Tiananmen Square.",
      "date": "1989-05-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tiananmen Square Massacre",
      "text": "On the night of June 3 and into the early morning of June 4, 1989, the Chinese government sent troops and tanks to clear Tiananmen Square, resulting in a violent crackdown known as the Tiananmen Square Massacre.",
      "date": "1989-06-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Aftermath and Global Reaction",
      "text": "Following the crackdown on June 4, 1989, there was widespread international condemnation and sanctions against the Chinese government.",
      "date": "1989-06-04"
    }
  ]
}

