      "text": "King David, who reigned from approximately 1010 to 970 BCE, is credited with uniting the tribes of Israel and establishing Jerusalem as its capital.",
      "date": "-1010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Solomon's Temple Built",
      "text": "King Solomon, son of David, built the First Temple in Jerusalem around 957 BCE, which became the central place of worship for the Jewish people.",
      "date": "-957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Division of the Kingdom",
      "text": "After Solomon's death, the kingdom split into the northern Kingdom of Israel and the southern Kingdom of Judah around 930 BCE.",
      "date": "-930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction of the Kingdom of Israel",
      "text": "The Assyrian Empire conquered the northern Kingdom of Israel in 722 BCE, leading to the exile of many Israelites.",
      "date": "-0722"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Babylonian Exile",
      "text": "In 586 BCE, the Babylonians destroyed Jerusalem and the First Temple, leading to the exile of the Jewish people to Babylon.",
      "date": "-0586"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return from Babylonian Exile",
      "text": "In 538 BCE, following the Persian conquest of Babylon, Jews were allowed to return to Judah and rebuild the Temple.",
      "date": "-0538"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Temple Completed",
      "text": "The Second Temple was completed in 516 BCE, marking the restoration of Jewish religious life in Jerusalem.",
      "date": "-0516"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Maccabean Revolt",
      "text": "The Maccabean Revolt against the Seleucid Empire occurred from 167 to 160 BCE, leading to the rededication of the Second Temple.",
      "date": "-0167"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction of the Second Temple",
      "text": "The Romans destroyed the Second Temple in 70 CE during the Siege of Jerusalem, a pivotal event in Jewish history.",
      "date": "0070"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bar Kokhba Revolt",
      "text": "The Bar Kokhba Revolt against Roman rule took place from 132 to 136 CE, resulting in significant Jewish casualties and diaspora.",
      "date": "0132"
