      "text": "The earthquake generated a massive tsunami with waves up to 30 meters high. The tsunami affected 14 countries, including Indonesia, Sri Lanka, India, and Thailand, causing widespread devastation.",
      "date": "2004-12-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Immediate Aftermath",
      "text": "In the immediate aftermath of the tsunami, over 230,000 people were killed, and millions were displaced across the affected regions. The disaster prompted a massive international humanitarian response.",
      "date": "2004-12-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Response",
      "text": "Countries around the world responded with humanitarian aid, sending supplies, medical teams, and financial assistance to the affected areas. The international community pledged billions of dollars in aid.",
      "date": "2004-12-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact on Infrastructure",
      "text": "The tsunami caused extensive damage to infrastructure, including homes, roads, and public facilities. Many coastal communities were completely destroyed, requiring long-term reconstruction efforts.",
      "date": "2004-12-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Environmental Impact",
      "text": "The tsunami also had significant environmental impacts, including the destruction of coral reefs, mangroves, and coastal ecosystems. The saltwater intrusion affected agriculture and freshwater supplies.",
      "date": "2004-12-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Tsunami Warning Systems",
      "text": "In response to the disaster, efforts were made to establish and improve tsunami warning systems in the Indian Ocean and other vulnerable regions to prevent future tragedies.",
      "date": "2005"
    }
  ]
}

