      "headline": "Composition of the Book of Enoch",
      "text": "The Book of Enoch, also known as 1 Enoch, is believed to have been composed between the 3rd century BCE and the 1st century CE. It is an ancient Jewish apocalyptic text attributed to Enoch, the great-grandfather of Noah.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inclusion in the Dead Sea Scrolls",
      "text": "Fragments of the Book of Enoch were found among the Dead Sea Scrolls, which were discovered in the Qumran Caves in the mid-20th century. These fragments date back to the 2nd century BCE.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Translation into Ge'ez",
      "text": "The Book of Enoch was translated into Ge'ez, an ancient South Semitic language, and became part of the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church's biblical canon. This translation is believed to have occurred between the 4th and 6th centuries CE.",
      "date": "0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rediscovery in Europe",
      "text": "The Book of Enoch was largely lost to Western Christianity until the 18th century when it was reintroduced to Europe by James Bruce, who brought back copies from Ethiopia in 1773.",
      "date": "1773"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First English Translation",
      "text": "The first complete English translation of the Book of Enoch was published in 1821 by Richard Laurence, an Oxford academic.",
      "date": "1821"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Scholarly Interest",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th and 21st centuries, the Book of Enoch has been the subject of increased scholarly interest, particularly in the fields of biblical studies and apocalyptic literature.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

