      "headline": "End of Reconstruction",
      "text": "The Reconstruction era ended in 1877, leading to the withdrawal of federal troops from the South and the beginning of the implementation of Jim Crow laws.",
      "date": "1877"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Plessy v. Ferguson",
      "text": "On May 18, 1896, the U.S. Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of racial segregation under the 'separate but equal' doctrine in the Plessy v. Ferguson case.",
      "date": "1896-05-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jim Crow Laws Enacted",
      "text": "From the late 19th century through the early 20th century, Southern states enacted Jim Crow laws to enforce racial segregation in public facilities and disenfranchise African Americans.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Brown v. Board of Education",
      "text": "On May 17, 1954, the U.S. Supreme Court declared state laws establishing separate public schools for black and white students to be unconstitutional, effectively overturning Plessy v. Ferguson.",
      "date": "1954-05-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Civil Rights Act of 1964",
      "text": "On July 2, 1964, the Civil Rights Act was signed into law, prohibiting discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex, or national origin, and ending segregation in public places.",
      "date": "1964-07-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Voting Rights Act of 1965",
      "text": "On August 6, 1965, the Voting Rights Act was signed into law, prohibiting racial discrimination in voting and effectively dismantling the legal basis for Jim Crow laws.",
      "date": "1965-08-06"
    }
  ]
}

