      "headline": "Introduction of Leap Year",
      "text": "The concept of a leap year was introduced by Julius Caesar in 45 BCE with the Julian calendar. February 29 was added as an intercalary day to keep the calendar year synchronized with the astronomical year.",
      "date": "-0045"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gregorian Calendar Reform",
      "text": "In 1582, Pope Gregory XIII introduced the Gregorian calendar, which refined the leap year system. February 29 continued to be used as the leap day, occurring in years divisible by 4, except for years divisible by 100 unless they are also divisible by 400.",
      "date": "1582"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Leap Year Day Celebrations",
      "text": "February 29 is often associated with various cultural traditions and celebrations. In some cultures, it is considered a day when women can propose marriage to men, a tradition that dates back to the 5th century.",
      "date": "0500"
    },
