{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "First Email System",
      "text": "In 1965, the first email system was developed at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) as part of a program called CTSS (Compatible Time-Sharing System). This system allowed multiple users to log into a central system from remote terminals and store files online.",
      "date": "1965"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of ARPANET",
      "text": "In 1969, ARPANET, the precursor to the modern internet, was established. It was a network that connected computers at different universities and research institutions, allowing them to communicate with each other.",
      "date": "1969"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ray Tomlinson Sends First Networked Email",
      "text": "In 1971, Ray Tomlinson, a computer engineer, sent the first networked email on ARPANET. He used the '@' symbol to separate the user's name from the computer name, which became the standard for email addresses.",
      "date": "1971"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Email Protocols",
      "text": "In the 1970s, several email protocols were developed, including SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) in 1982, which became the standard protocol for sending emails.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of MIME",
      "text": "In 1991, MIME (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) was introduced, allowing emails to include multimedia content such as images, audio, and video.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of Web-based Email Services",
      "text": "In the mid-1990s, web-based email services like Hotmail (launched in 1996) and Yahoo Mail (launched in 1997) became popular, allowing users to access their email from any web browser.",
      "date": "1996"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Gmail",
      "text": "In 2004, Google launched Gmail, offering users 1 GB of storage space, which was significantly more than its competitors at the time. Gmail's introduction marked a significant shift in email services.",
      "date": "2004"
    }
  ]
}

