      "headline": "Start of the Rwandan Genocide",
      "text": "The Rwandan genocide began on April 7, 1994, following the assassination of Rwandan President Juvénal Habyarimana. This event triggered the mass killings of Tutsi ethnic group members, as well as moderate Hutu and Twa.",
      "date": "1994-04-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Assassination of President Juvénal Habyarimana",
      "text": "On April 6, 1994, the plane carrying Rwandan President Juvénal Habyarimana was shot down, killing him. This assassination is widely considered the catalyst for the genocide.",
      "date": "1994-04-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mass Killings and Violence",
      "text": "Throughout the genocide, which lasted approximately 100 days, members of the Tutsi ethnic group, as well as moderate Hutu and Twa, were systematically killed by Hutu militias. The violence was marked by extreme brutality, with victims often murdered by neighbors.",
      "date": "1994-04 to 1994-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Sexual Violence",
      "text": "During the genocide, between 250,000 and 500,000 women were subjected to sexual violence, including rape, as part of the systematic campaign of terror against the Tutsi population.",
      "date": "1994-04 to 1994-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Rwandan Genocide",
      "text": "The genocide officially ended on July 19, 1994, when the Rwandan Patriotic Front (RPF), a Tutsi-led rebel group, took control of the country, effectively stopping the mass killings.",
      "date": "1994-07-19"
    }
  ]
}

