      "text": "Calvin Coolidge married Grace Anna Goodhue on October 4, 1905.",
      "date": "1905-10-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Elected Governor of Massachusetts",
      "text": "Coolidge was elected as the 48th Governor of Massachusetts, serving from 1919 to 1921.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Boston Police Strike",
      "text": "In 1919, Coolidge gained national attention for his handling of the Boston Police Strike, asserting that there is no right to strike against the public safety by anybody, anywhere, anytime.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Elected Vice President of the United States",
      "text": "Coolidge was elected as the 29th Vice President of the United States, serving under President Warren G. Harding from 1921 to 1923.",
      "date": "1921"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Becomes President of the United States",
      "text": "Calvin Coolidge became the 30th President of the United States on August 2, 1923, following the death of President Warren G. Harding.",
      "date": "1923-08-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Immigration Act of 1924",
      "text": "Coolidge signed the Immigration Act of 1924, which limited the number of immigrants allowed entry into the United States through a national origins quota.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reelection as President",
      "text": "Coolidge was elected to a full term as President in 1924, serving until 1929.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kellogg-Briand Pact",
      "text": "In 1928, Coolidge signed the Kellogg-Briand Pact, which renounced war as an instrument of national policy.",
      "date": "1928"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Presidency",
      "text": "Coolidge's presidency ended on March 4, 1929, when Herbert Hoover succeeded him.",
      "date": "1929-03-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Calvin Coolidge",
      "text": "Calvin Coolidge died on January 5, 1933, in Northampton, Massachusetts.",
      "date": "1933-01-05"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Birth of John D. Rockefeller",
      "text": "John Davison Rockefeller was born on July 8, 1839, in Richford, New York.",
      "date": "1839-07-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Job as an Assistant Bookkeeper",
      "text": "In 1855, at the age of 16, Rockefeller became an assistant bookkeeper at Hewitt & Tuttle, a small produce commission firm in Cleveland, Ohio.",
      "date": "1855"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Business Partnership",
      "text": "In 1859, at the age of 20, Rockefeller went into his first business partnership with Maurice B. Clark, focusing on produce and commission.",
      "date": "1859"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Entry into the Oil Industry",
      "text": "In 1863, Rockefeller entered the oil business by investing in a Cleveland refinery.",
      "date": "1863"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding of Standard Oil Company",
      "text": "On January 10, 1870, Rockefeller founded the Standard Oil Company, which became the largest oil refinery firm in the world.",
      "date": "1870-01-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Standard Oil Trust Formation",
      "text": "In 1882, Rockefeller created the Standard Oil Trust, a business trust that centralized control over many oil companies.",
      "date": "1882"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retirement from Standard Oil",
      "text": "In 1897, Rockefeller retired from the day-to-day operations of Standard Oil but remained its largest shareholder.",
      "date": "1897"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Philanthropic Focus",
      "text": "After retiring, Rockefeller focused on philanthropy, contributing to education, medicine, and scientific research, including the founding of the University of Chicago in 1890 and Rockefeller University in 1901.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dissolution of Standard Oil",
      "text": "In 1911, the U.S. Supreme Court found Standard Oil in violation of antitrust laws and ordered its dissolution into 34 smaller companies.",
      "date": "1911"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of John D. Rockefeller",
      "text": "John D. Rockefeller died on May 23, 1937, in Ormond Beach, Florida, at the age of 97.",
      "date": "1937-05-23"
    }
  ]
}

