      "headline": "Formation of Austria-Hungary",
      "text": "The Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867 established the dual monarchy of Austria-Hungary, following the Austro-Prussian War. This agreement created two separate states, Austria and Hungary, under a single monarch.",
      "date": "1867"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Franz Joseph I Becomes Emperor and King",
      "text": "Franz Joseph I, who had been Emperor of Austria since 1848, became the King of Hungary as well, ruling over the newly formed Austria-Hungary from 1867.",
      "date": "1867"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bosnian Crisis",
      "text": "In 1908, Austria-Hungary formally annexed Bosnia and Herzegovina, which had been under its administration since 1878, leading to tensions with Serbia and Russia.",
      "date": "1908"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand",
      "text": "On June 28, 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, was assassinated in Sarajevo. This event triggered the outbreak of World War I.",
      "date": "1914-06-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War I",
      "text": "Austria-Hungary was a central power during World War I, which lasted from 1914 to 1918. The empire faced significant military and economic challenges during the war.",
      "date": "1914"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Emperor Franz Joseph I",
      "text": "Emperor Franz Joseph I died on November 21, 1916, after a reign of 68 years. He was succeeded by his grandnephew, Charles I.",
      "date": "1916-11-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dissolution of Austria-Hungary",
      "text": "Following the end of World War I, Austria-Hungary was dissolved in 1918. The empire's territories were divided into several independent countries, including Austria, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, and Yugoslavia.",
      "date": "1918"
    }
  ]
}

