      "headline": "Diwali Festival Begins",
      "text": "Diwali, also known as Deepavali, is a major Hindu festival celebrated over five days. It typically begins on the 13th day of the dark fortnight of the Hindu lunisolar month of Ashvin, which falls between mid-September and mid-November.",
      "date": "0000-10"  },
    {
      "headline": "Dhanteras",
      "text": "The first day of Diwali is known as Dhanteras. It is dedicated to prosperity and is considered an auspicious day for buying gold, silver, and other valuables. This day marks the beginning of the festival.",
      "date": "0000-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naraka Chaturdashi",
      "text": "The second day of Diwali is Naraka Chaturdashi, also known as Choti Diwali. It commemorates the victory of Lord Krishna over the demon Narakasura.",
      "date": "0000-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lakshmi Puja",
      "text": "The third day is the main day of Diwali, known as Lakshmi Puja. It is dedicated to the goddess Lakshmi, the goddess of wealth and prosperity. People light lamps and candles to welcome her into their homes.",
      "date": "0000-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Govardhan Puja",
      "text": "The fourth day of Diwali is Govardhan Puja, also known as Annakut. It is celebrated in honor of Lord Krishna, who lifted the Govardhan Hill to protect villagers from torrential rains.",
      "date": "0000-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bhai Dooj",
      "text": "The fifth and final day of Diwali is Bhai Dooj, a day dedicated to the bond between brothers and sisters. Sisters pray for their brothers' well-being and perform aarti, while brothers give gifts in return.",
      "date": "0000-10"
    }
  ]
}

