      "headline": "First Description of Parkinson's Disease",
      "text": "In 1817, James Parkinson, an English surgeon, published 'An Essay on the Shaking Palsy', which was the first detailed description of the condition that would later bear his name.",
      "date": "1817"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Parkinson's Disease",
      "text": "In 1872, French neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot proposed that the disease be named after James Parkinson, recognizing his contributions to the understanding of the condition.",
      "date": "1872"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Dopamine Deficiency",
      "text": "In the 1950s, researchers discovered that Parkinson's disease is associated with a deficiency of dopamine in the brain, particularly in the substantia nigra.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Levodopa",
      "text": "In the late 1960s, Levodopa (L-DOPA) was introduced as a treatment for Parkinson's disease, significantly improving symptoms for many patients.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Deep Brain Stimulation",
      "text": "In the 1980s, deep brain stimulation (DBS) was developed as a surgical treatment for Parkinson's disease, providing relief for patients with advanced symptoms.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Approval of DBS by FDA",
      "text": "In 1997, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved deep brain stimulation for the treatment of Parkinson's disease.",
      "date": "1997"
    }
  ]
