      "headline": "Gabapentin Development",
      "text": "Gabapentin was developed in the 1970s by the Japanese company Parke-Davis, a subsidiary of Warner-Lambert, as a muscle relaxant and an anti-spasmodic agent.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Epilepsy",
      "text": "In 1993, Gabapentin was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for use as an adjunctive medication to control partial seizures in adults with epilepsy.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Postherpetic Neuralgia",
      "text": "In 2002, Gabapentin received FDA approval for the treatment of postherpetic neuralgia, a type of neuropathic pain that occurs after shingles.",
      "date": "2002"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Patent Expiry and Generic Availability",
      "text": "The patent for Gabapentin expired in 2004, allowing generic versions of the drug to become available, which significantly reduced the cost of the medication.",
      "date": "2004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Off-label Use and Legal Issues",
      "text": "Gabapentin has been widely used off-label for various conditions, leading to legal issues. In 2004, Warner-Lambert paid $430 million to resolve criminal and civil charges that it had illegally promoted Gabapentin for unapproved uses.",
      "date": "2004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Increased Use for Neuropathic Pain",
      "text": "By the 2010s, Gabapentin became one of the most commonly prescribed medications for neuropathic pain, including diabetic neuropathy and central pain.",
      "date": "2010"
    }
  ]
}

