      "headline": "Origin of Seppuku",
      "text": "Seppuku, also known as harakiri, originated as a form of Japanese ritualistic suicide by disembowelment. It was initially reserved for samurai as part of their code of honor.",
      "date": "-1180"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Recorded Instance",
      "text": "The first recorded instance of seppuku was in 1180, when Minamoto no Yorimasa committed seppuku after his defeat in the Battle of Uji.",
      "date": "1180"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formalization during the Edo Period",
      "text": "During the Edo period (1603–1868), seppuku became a formalized ritual, often involving a second (kaishakunin) who would decapitate the individual after the initial cut to minimize suffering.",
      "date": "1603"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Meiji Restoration and Decline of Samurai",
      "text": "The Meiji Restoration in 1868 led to the decline of the samurai class and the abolition of the feudal system, reducing the practice of seppuku.",
      "date": "1868"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Seppuku in the Shōwa Era",
      "text": "During the Shōwa era (1926–1989), seppuku was practiced by some Japanese people to restore honor for themselves or their families, notably during World War II.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Yukio Mishima's Seppuku",
      "text": "On November 25, 1970, the famous Japanese author Yukio Mishima committed seppuku after a failed coup attempt, bringing international attention to the ritual.",
      "date": "1970-11-25"
    }
  ]
}

