      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita is believed to have been composed between the 2nd century BCE and the 1st century BCE. It is part of the Indian epic Mahabharata and is a 700-verse Hindu scripture that is a conversation between Prince Arjuna and the god Krishna, who serves as his charioteer.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Integration into the Mahabharata",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita is integrated into the Mahabharata, specifically in the Bhishma Parva (Book of Bhishma), chapters 23–40. The Mahabharata itself was composed over several centuries, with the Gita being one of its later additions.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Philosophical Synthesis",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita synthesizes various strands of Indian religious thought, including the Vedic concept of dharma, samkhya-based yoga and jnana (knowledge), and bhakti (devotion). This synthesis has contributed to its unique pan-Hindu influence.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence on Hindu Philosophy",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita has had a profound influence on Hindu philosophy, particularly in the Vedanta and Vaishnava traditions. It is considered one of the most important texts in Hinduism, providing guidance on how to live a life of righteousness and devotion.",
      "date": "-0200"
    }
  ]
}

