      "text": "Idi Amin was promoted to the rank of lieutenant in 1961, becoming one of the first two Ugandans to become commissioned officers.",
      "date": "1961"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ugandan Independence",
      "text": "Uganda gained independence from Britain on October 9, 1962. Idi Amin was promoted to captain and then to major in the newly formed Ugandan Army.",
      "date": "1962-10-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Becomes Deputy Commander of the Army",
      "text": "In 1964, Idi Amin was appointed as the deputy commander of the Ugandan Army.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Involvement in the Gold Scandal",
      "text": "In 1966, Idi Amin was implicated in a gold smuggling scandal, which strained his relationship with Prime Minister Milton Obote.",
      "date": "1966"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coup d'état and Presidency",
      "text": "On January 25, 1971, Idi Amin led a military coup that overthrew President Milton Obote. He declared himself President of Uganda.",
      "date": "1971-01-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expulsion of Asians",
      "text": "In August 1972, Idi Amin ordered the expulsion of Asians from Uganda, giving them 90 days to leave the country.",
      "date": "1972-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Entebbe Hostage Crisis",
      "text": "On July 4, 1976, Israeli commandos carried out a successful raid at Entebbe Airport to rescue hostages held by pro-Palestinian hijackers, with Idi Amin's support.",
      "date": "1976-07-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Overthrown from Power",
      "text": "Idi Amin was overthrown on April 11, 1979, by Ugandan exiles and Tanzanian forces. He fled to Libya and then to Saudi Arabia.",
      "date": "1979-04-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Idi Amin",
      "text": "Idi Amin died on August 16, 2003, in Jeddah, Saudi Arabia.",
      "date": "2003-08-16"
    }
  ]
}

