      "headline": "Start of the Osage Indian Murders",
      "text": "The Osage Indian murders began in Osage County, Oklahoma, during the 1910s, as wealthy Osage individuals started to die under suspicious circumstances.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of the Murders",
      "text": "The majority of the murders occurred between 1921 and 1926, during which time at least 60 wealthy, full-blood Osage individuals were reported killed.",
      "date": "1921"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Investigation by the FBI",
      "text": "In response to the increasing number of unsolved murders, the FBI, led by J. Edgar Hoover, launched an investigation into the Osage murders.",
      "date": "1925"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Conviction of William Hale",
      "text": "William Hale, a prominent local figure, was convicted in 1929 for orchestrating the murders to gain control of oil-rich land owned by the Osage.",
      "date": "1929"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Reign of Terror",
      "text": "The period known as the 'Reign of Terror' effectively ended in the early 1930s, although investigations suggest that the death toll may have been much higher than initially reported.",
      "date": "1931"
    }
  ]
}

