      "headline": "Investigation and Legal Consequences",
      "text": "Following the death of Kenneth Pinyan, an investigation was launched which revealed a group of men involved in zoophilic activities at the farm. James Michael Tait was charged with trespassing, as bestiality was not illegal in Washington state at the time. The case led to public outcry and legal changes.",
      "date": "2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Washington State Law Change",
      "text": "In response to the Enumclaw horse sex case, Washington state passed a bill in 2006 making bestiality a felony. The law was enacted to prevent similar incidents and address the legal loophole that allowed such activities to go unpunished.",
