      "text": "In 1770, Lieutenant James Cook and the crew of HMS Endeavour became the first Europeans to chart the eastern coast of Australia, including the area that would become Sydney.",
      "date": "1770"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Fleet Arrival",
      "text": "On January 26, 1788, the First Fleet, led by Captain Arthur Phillip, arrived at Port Jackson, establishing the first European colony in Australia, which would become Sydney.",
      "date": "1788-01-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Sydney",
      "text": "The settlement was named Sydney in honor of the then British Home Secretary, Thomas Townshend, Lord Sydney, in 1788.",
      "date": "1788"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rum Rebellion",
      "text": "In 1808, the Rum Rebellion occurred, the only successful armed takeover of government in Australian history, led by the New South Wales Corps against Governor William Bligh.",
      "date": "1808"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sydney Incorporation",
      "text": "Sydney was incorporated as a city in 1842, marking its official recognition as a city.",
      "date": "1842"
    },
    {
