      "headline": "Millerite Movement Begins",
      "text": "The Millerite movement, which would eventually lead to the formation of the Seventh-day Adventist Church, began in the early 19th century. William Miller, a Baptist preacher, started preaching about the imminent Second Coming of Jesus Christ.",
      "date": "1830"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Disappointment",
      "text": "On October 22, 1844, the followers of William Miller experienced the 'Great Disappointment' when Jesus did not return as predicted. This event led to a reevaluation of beliefs and the eventual formation of the Seventh-day Adventist Church.",
      "date": "1844-10-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sabbath Observance Adopted",
      "text": "In the mid-1840s, a group of Millerites began observing the Sabbath on Saturday, the seventh day of the week, which became a distinguishing feature of the Seventh-day Adventist Church.",
      "date": "1845"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ellen G. White's Vision",
      "text": "Ellen G. White, one of the co-founders of the Seventh-day Adventist Church, had her first vision in December 1844, which played a significant role in shaping the beliefs and practices of the church.",
      "date": "1844-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formal Establishment of the Church",
      "text": "The Seventh-day Adventist Church was formally established on May 21, 1863, in Battle Creek, Michigan, with a membership of about 3,500.",
      "date": "1863-05-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ellen G. White's Death",
      "text": "Ellen G. White, a prominent figure in the Seventh-day Adventist Church, passed away on July 16, 1915. Her writings continue to influence the church.",
      "date": "1915-07-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "General Conference Session in San Francisco",
      "text": "In 1918, the General Conference Session was held in San Francisco, marking a significant event in the church's history.",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adventist Development and Relief Agency (ADRA) Founded",
      "text": "The Adventist Development and Relief Agency (ADRA) was founded in 1956, becoming a major humanitarian arm of the Seventh-day Adventist Church.",
      "date": "1956"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Seventh-day Adventist Church Membership Reaches 1 Million",
      "text": "In 1955, the membership of the Seventh-day Adventist Church reached 1 million worldwide, marking a significant milestone in its growth.",
      "date": "1955"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Seventh-day Adventist Church Membership Reaches 10 Million",
