      "headline": "Served in the Continental Congress",
      "text": "Madison served in the Continental Congress from 1780 to 1783, where he became known for his leadership and advocacy for a strong federal government.",
      "date": "1780"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Virginia Plan and Constitutional Convention",
      "text": "In 1787, Madison played a key role in the Constitutional Convention, presenting the Virginia Plan and contributing significantly to the drafting of the U.S. Constitution.",
      "date": "1787"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Federalist Papers Publication",
      "text": "Between 1787 and 1788, Madison co-authored the Federalist Papers with Alexander Hamilton and John Jay, advocating for the ratification of the Constitution.",
      "date": "1787"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Elected to the U.S. House of Representatives",
      "text": "In 1789, Madison was elected to the first U.S. House of Representatives, where he sponsored the Bill of Rights.",
      "date": "1789"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Secretary of State Appointment",
      "text": "In 1801, Madison was appointed as Secretary of State by President Thomas Jefferson, serving until 1809.",
      "date": "1801"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inauguration as Fourth President of the United States",
      "text": "James Madison was inaugurated as the fourth President of the United States on March 4, 1809.",
      "date": "1809-03-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "War of 1812",
      "text": "During Madison's presidency, the War of 1812 broke out between the United States and Great Britain, lasting from 1812 to 1815.",
      "date": "1812"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Presidency",
      "text": "Madison's presidency ended on March 4, 1817, after serving two terms.",
      "date": "1817-03-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retirement to Montpelier",
      "text": "After his presidency, Madison retired to his estate, Montpelier, in Virginia.",
      "date": "1817"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of James Madison",
      "text": "James Madison died on June 28, 1836, at his Montpelier estate in Virginia.",
