      "headline": "Circuit Judge",
      "text": "Wallace served as a judge of the Third Judicial Circuit of Alabama from 1953 to 1959.",
      "date": "1953"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Term as Governor of Alabama",
      "text": "Wallace was inaugurated as the 45th governor of Alabama on January 14, 1963, where he famously declared 'segregation now, segregation tomorrow, segregation forever.'",
      "date": "1963-01-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Stand in the Schoolhouse Door",
      "text": "On June 11, 1963, Wallace stood at the door of the University of Alabama to block the entry of two African American students, an event known as the 'Stand in the Schoolhouse Door.'",
      "date": "1963-06-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1964 Presidential Campaign",
      "text": "Wallace ran for the Democratic nomination for President of the United States in 1964, gaining significant support in the North.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1968 Presidential Campaign",
      "text": "Wallace ran for President as the candidate of the American Independent Party in 1968, carrying five Southern states.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Term as Governor of Alabama",
      "text": "Wallace began his second term as governor on January 18, 1971.",
      "date": "1971-01-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1972 Presidential Campaign and Assassination Attempt",
      "text": "During his 1972 presidential campaign, Wallace was shot and paralyzed from the waist down on May 15, 1972.",
      "date": "1972-05-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Third Term as Governor of Alabama",
      "text": "Wallace began his third term as governor on January 15, 1975.",
      "date": "1975-01-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fourth Term as Governor of Alabama",
      "text": "Wallace began his fourth and final term as governor on January 17, 1983.",
      "date": "1983-01-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renunciation of Segregationist Views",
      "text": "In the late 1970s, Wallace publicly renounced his earlier segregationist views and sought reconciliation with civil rights leaders.",
